# This repository stores files exactly as written, with CRLF endings.
#
# The tooling is Windows PowerShell, and the active hooks under .claude/hooks
# are byte-compared against their packaged copies under claude-hooks. Any
# end-of-line conversion would break that parity check and rewrite every blob.
#
# The initial baseline was committed with CRLF already in the objects, so with
# a global core.autocrlf=true and core.safecrlf=true, git refuses the round trip
# and no further commit is possible. Disabling text conversion resolves that
# without rewriting the existing history.

* -text

# Shell scripts are executed on Ubuntu hosts. A CR byte makes bash fail with
# "$'\r': command not found", so these are normalised to LF in the object store
# and checked out as LF regardless of the platform that wrote them.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Ledger and provenance artifacts are pinned by sha256 and cited by hash in
# execution briefs. Under the blanket "* -text" rule above, an editor that
# re-saves one of these with Windows line endings changes every byte-line and
# breaks the pin while altering no data — which is exactly what happened to
# manifestv3.1resolved.jsonl and halted the LangGraph pilot at pre-flight.
#
# Normalising to LF in the object store makes the hash stable regardless of the
# tool that wrote the file. All four tracked .jsonl artifacts are already LF, so
# this changes no content and no existing hash. It does not touch the hook
# byte-parity check, which covers .py and .ps1 only.
*.jsonl text eol=lf
